From: pavel@PARK.bu.edu (Paul Cisek)
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 1994 15:29:18 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-379] Resend Thread 'Liquid Sentience' #94-9-219 - #94-9-239

>From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
>Subject: [#94-9-219] Liquid Sentience
>
>I have been reading the list for several months now, and have observed many
>discussions about possible directions for the evolution of human
>consciousness, AI, or some sort of synergism between them.  However, I have
>been surprised to find that no one has mentioned what seems to me to be an
>obvious possibility... that the notion of sentience will cease to have
>meaning in terms of discrete entities, but rather be described as a
>continuum or liquid.

Actually, Alexander Chislenko recently discussed this very issue in a post
entitled `Networking in the Mind Age'.  It was posted to the Exi-essay list,
which you might not receive.  (The exi-essay list is used for posting lengthy
and well thought out articles.  It is of very low volume and highly recommended
(at least by me).  See your welcome message for subscription info).

>Certainly our biological past and the grammatical structure of our language
>have a lot to do with this assumption -- but I don't see any a priori
>reason why this notion of *individuality* would necessarily be retained in
>the future.  It seems to derive from our biological roots as sentience
>evolving in separate, non-miscible physical bodies and minds.  However, as
>soon as it is possible to meaningfully discuss the possibility of
>"uploads", or of transferring our consciousness to "artificial brains", the
>need for individuality seems to vanish.

Yes, this is quite possible.  However, our current experience with knowledge
seems to suggest that meaning derives from an interconnectedness among
concepts.  Such associations are constructed through experience, upon which
resulting semantics are grounded.  Thus the only example we have of
intelligence is one where individuality falls out of an experiencial focus that
underlies knowledge.  Perhaps this is caused by historical reasons.  We evolved
as separate organisms and thus had separate experiences and thus have separate
foundations of meaning.  Perhaps entities who are motivationally coupled and
can share experiences (like those described by Alex) would construct a liquid
sentience of sorts.  Even so, might there exist a positive feedback that
focuses distributed viewpoints into what eventually becomes a single
super-organism?


>From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
>Subject: [#94-9-229] Liquid Sentience
>
>More complex consciousness implies increased differentiation of function.
>Function always points to purpose, so more complex consciousness will have more
>purposes.  What will the cause of the new purposes be?  What will keep the new
>purposes out of conflict?  Only an individual can accomplish this generation and
>harmonizing of purposes prior to action.  I doubt that natural selection will
>allow parts to make conflicting action decisions and remain coherent over long
>temporal spans.

Actually, we see examples of this in nature all the time.  Consider the
host-parasite relationship where each has learned to live with each other
to the extent where biologists can no longer tell where one organism ends and
another begins.

>It is better to have many autonomous units pointing in
>every direction, some of them will be right.  The right direction is
>transcomputable, the future is indeterminate in this aspect.  Ambiguity is what
>the environment presents to us and undecidibility choices are rampant.  Through
>individuality, nature provides for a multiplicity of directional purposes to
>maximize long-term order in the face of an unknown future, rather than putting
>all the eggs in one functionally integrated basket that may be headed off a
>cliff.

This "many baskets" strategy sort of falls out of the evolutionary process.
Evolution requires variations to select among.

Paul


