From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 01:39:48 GMT
Subject: [#94-9-386] Quantum Erasers? (fwd)

Nick Szabo writes:
> a forwarded article describes the quantum eraser:
> > You have one
> > laser split into four beams. Two are brought together on one
> > screen, and the other two on a distant screen. If one of the
> > four paths are blocked, you would expect the interference
> > pattern to go away on that one screen. The good part is that the
> > interference goes away on both screens. That means the photons
> > affect each other even though there is no physical connection.
> 
> Does this allow instantaneous transmission of information?

No, because the display of the interference pattern is delayed by the
time it takes light to pass from the blocker to the interference screen. 
(I am looking at the four beam experiment, top diagram page 77, Sci Am
July 1992.)  So any transmission takes place at light speed, minus the
slight delay due to the angle of the beams.

> For instance, let's say I split a laser into 4 beams, two
> brought together onto a screen orbiting Earth, and two
> brought together on a screen orbiting Mars.  After the
> beams have been set up (several minutes have passed to allow the
> beam to reach Mars), I start blocking one of the beams at
> Earth, erasing the interference pattern on the Earth orbiting
> screen.  By the above description, the interference pattern will 
> also go away on the screen at Mars.  Does this happen instantaneously,
> or (with the laser beam originating at Earth) do I have to wait 
> for the correlated photons to get to Mars?

You have to wait.  The blocker has to be interposed early in the split
beam path and the delay before the interference effects are observed is
simply the time taken for the light to travel the rest of the arc path.

> How about if I place the laser beam halfway between Earth and
> Mars, but block the beam at Earth?  In this case correlated
> photons arrive simultaneously at Earth and Mars.

In the setup I am looking at the photons always have to travel from the
blocker to the interference site, so no FTL effects can possibily be
manifest.

> I understand that a similar setup can allow the instantaneous
> (ie, infinitely faster than light) "transmission" of correlated 
> random data: "quantum cryptography".

No, quantum cryptography (I think, I'd have to check to be sure) and
quantum teleportation (I know) both rely on an accompanying classical
signal transmission, which is necessarily not FTL.

