From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 1994 22:28:10 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-389] SCI: Role for Q.M. in neurobiology

>From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
>X-Message-Number: #94-9-378
>
>First, these are by no means "Penrose's" constructs.

I'm only familiar with Penrose's argument, so it's the only one I can
really criticize.  However, I will also criticize any arguments that
fail to provide good justification for why their proposed mechanisms
should be found in biology.  If there are "quantum biology" theories
that answer this question then I'd be interested in hearing about them...

>Perhaps the early
>mechanisms for intracellular organization based on some QM process (e.g.
>the microtubule hypothesis) laid the groundwork for extended structures
>which coordinate *between* cells.

Yes, perhaps.  But while "perhaps" is good for speculation, it's not
sufficient for a scientific theory.  It is as a scientific theory that
I criticize Penrose's microtubule hypothesis (was it Hameroff that originally
proposed it?).

But it seems we agree that the question me must ask is not if QM might
be necessary for consciousness, but whether it might be necessary for
cell function.  That I have not seen addressed adequately.  Do other
"quantum biology" theories make any significant arguments on this issue?

>Speaking as a biologist (a title which I can loosely claim), I
>can assure you that the details of intracellular organization are some of
>the least well understood subjects in molecular biology.

So what do the cell biologists think of the microtubule hypothesis?
Why would a conformational change governed by quantum effects be functionally
superior to one governed by enzyme action?  I would suspect that enzyme
action offers far greater sophistication of control than a coherence
phenomenon.  Again, I can accept the QM explanation, but not in the
absence of any good argument for why it should be relevant.  That it
might is just not enough - there are plenty of theories out there to
consider.

>It is not necessary to assume that "neural networks" came first, and that
>some Q.M. process was only tacked on later when it was needed.  It could be
>that the functioning of the brain is only based on neural networks at a
>gross macroscopic scale, and that these underlying processes have *always*
>been present.  In fact, I would predict that *if* something of this sort is
>shown to be occurring in vertebrate brains, that it also occurs in some
>fashion throughout the evolutionary tree.

I agree.  I think that any mechanisms that occur at such a fundamental level
are likely to have evolved early, just because it would be hard for them to
evolve later on.  The mutational cost of redesigning at such a level makes it
rather an unlikely scenario.  I was pleased to hear that Penrose suggested that
if indeed these effects are important for explaining human behavior, then we
must expect to find them important in more primitive behavior as well.

(I understand that this is speculation.  But if it is to be at all useful
then it must face critical evaluation.)

Paul


